  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil conditions are anticipated through the\ndaytime hours Saturday as high pressure centered north of the \nregion slides slowly eastward.\n\nMid-level ridging over portions of the Gulf Coast region \nnortheastward into the Mid-Atlantic today will continue to shift\nslowly southwestward, with northwesterly flow aloft increasing \nacross the area tonight through Saturday. At the surface, High \npressure currently centered over portions of Ontario will shift \neastward through Saturday, passing north of the region on \nSaturday before shifting offshore. \n\nA warm and dry afternoon is in store with some clouds around. \nTemperatures have warmed into the 70s to near 80 across the \narea, with a few more degrees of warming anticipated through \nthis afternoon. Tonight, low temperatures will range from near \n50 across portions of eastern PA and northern NJ to the mid 60s \nacross portions of Delmarva as a cooler airmass starts to settle\ninto the region. It should remain dry through the daytime hours\nSaturday, though some clouds will remain around. It should be \nnotably cooler however, with high temperatures generally in the \nlow to mid 70s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A series of cold fronts and low pressure \nsystems will keep unsettled weather over the area from Saturday \nnight through Thursday. Heavy rain is possible Sunday and Sunday\nnight.\n\nHigh pressure moves out to sea Saturday night, and then low \npressure organizes and develops over the Midwest. A warm front \nextending out from that low will lift north through the \nNortheast Saturday night through Sunday morning. Showers first \ndevelop after midnight Saturday night and continue through \nSunday morning.\n\nAs low pressure draws nearer to the region, southerly flow \nincreases throughout the Northeast and low level moisture \nincreases as well with surface dew points rising well into the \n60s and low 70s and PWATs will be in excess of 2 inches. Low \npressure then passes through the southern Poconos and Lehigh \nValley Sunday afternoon and evening, resulting in more \nwidespread showers with isolated thunderstorms across most of \nthe area during that time. Locally heavy rain may develop from \ntime to time. As that low passes through the region, a cold \nfront will sag south through the region Sunday night, and the \naxis of heavy rain will then track south towards the I-95 \ncorridor and into the Philadelphia area. The front will stall \nout over Delmarva Monday morning.\n\nWPC continues the Slight Risk for Excessive Rainfall (2 out of \n4) for Sunday and Sunday night for areas along the I-95 corridor\nand points north and west. A Marginal Risk remains for the rest\nof the region south and east of the I-95 corridor. A Marginal \nRisk also continues for the entire region into Monday.\n\nSeveral more disturbances will pass through the region Monday \nand Tuesday, and this will keep rain chances in the forecast \nduring this time. For now, PoPs are limited to 30 to 50 percent \n(chance) given the uncertainty of the timing and coverage of \nshowers. Low pressure looks to develop over the Mid-Atlantic \ncoast and will meander over the western Atlantic between the \nJersey Shore and south of Long Island during the midweek period.\nOnshore flow and unsettled weather continues with below normal \ntemperatures.",
